# Template for apps/web/.env.production — copy to that name and fill in.
#
# The real file is gitignored and must stay that way. It was committed once, in
# ac840fe, to stop a deploy shipping a blank browser config; that turned out to
# be treating the symptom (see WHY IT IS NOT COMMITTED below) and the values are
# now held in the host instead.
#
# WHAT THESE ARE
#
# The public half of the browser bundle's configuration. next.config.ts inlines
# every value here into the JavaScript served to visitors, so none of it can be
# secret in the first place — but that is not a reason to commit it, only a
# reason that committing it was not a disclosure.
#
# ONLY PUBLIC VALUES BELONG IN THIS FILE. Server-side secrets — the Firebase
# *admin* credentials, database URLs, the Fapshi API key, anything that
# authorises rather than identifies — must never be added here. Those belong in
# the host's own secret store and nowhere in git.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT COMMITTED
#
# A production build with these unset does not fail. It silently bakes in each
# read site's `||` fallback and ships `apiKey: ""` with an API base of
# http://localhost:8000 — which reaches users as a sign-in button that cannot
# work. Committing the file did prevent that, but the actual cause was
# elsewhere: turbo.json's build task did not declare `KORAA_PUBLIC_*` in its
# `env`, and Turborepo's strict env mode drops undeclared variables before
# `next build` starts. All ten values were set in Vercel's dashboard and every
# one was stripped, so the file was covering for a build that could not see the
# host's configuration at all.
#
# turbo.json now declares `KORAA_PUBLIC_*` with a wildcard, so the host's values
# reach the build and this file is no longer load-bearing. Two things to keep in
# mind because of that history:
#
#   - A variable added under a *different* prefix needs a turbo.json entry of
#     its own, or it will be silently stripped exactly as these were.
#   - next.config.ts warns at build time when a required value is missing, and
#     KORAA_STRICT_PUBLIC_ENV=1 turns that warning into a build failure. Use it
#     in a release job that has nothing live to fall back to.
#
# WHERE THE REAL VALUES LIVE
#
#   Vercel: Project Settings -> Environment Variables, ticked for Production and
#           Preview. Values added there do not reach a build that already ran.
#   Docker: --build-arg, see apps/web/Dockerfile and
#           infrastructure/docker/docker-compose.prod.yml.
#   Local:  apps/web/.env.local — gitignored, and it outranks this file, so
#           `npm run dev` keeps your localhost values.
#
# PRECEDENCE — Next loads, highest priority first:
#   1. the real environment   <- Vercel/Docker vars land here and WIN
#   2. .env.production.local
#   3. .env.local             <- gitignored; local dev keeps localhost values
#   4. .env.production        <- the file this templates
#   5. .env

# ── API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# https, not http: the pages are served over https and browsers block mixed
# content, so an http base fails every request before it is sent. The deployed
# backend uses an sslip.io name so EC2 can hold a publicly trusted certificate
# without a purchased domain — see infrastructure/aws/setup-letsencrypt.sh.
KORAA_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://<api-host>/api/v1

# ── Origins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The platform's own host, as opposed to a merchant storefront domain.
# proxy.ts treats *.vercel.app as a platform host rather than a store slug, so
# /auth/* and /dashboard/* serve normally there instead of being rewritten onto
# a storefront.
KORAA_PUBLIC_ROOT_DOMAIN=<your-app>.vercel.app
KORAA_PUBLIC_DASHBOARD_ORIGIN=https://<your-app>.vercel.app
KORAA_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://<your-app>.vercel.app

# ── Firebase (client SDK — public, see the note at the top) ───────────────────
# Firebase Console -> Project settings -> Your apps -> SDK setup and config.
# Access is not controlled by these values; it is controlled by Security Rules
# and the Authorized-domains list, which is why Google prints them in its own
# setup snippets. The host serving the app MUST be in Authorized domains or
# Google sign-in fails with auth/unauthorized-domain.
KORAA_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=AIza...
KORAA_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=<project-id>.firebaseapp.com
KORAA_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=<project-id>
KORAA_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=<project-id>.firebasestorage.app
KORAA_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=<sender-id>
KORAA_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:<sender-id>:web:<app-id>
